# What a source archive of this repository contains.
#
# `export-ignore` applies to `git archive`, which is what GitHub serves as "Download
# ZIP" and as the source tarball attached to every release and tag. It does not touch
# the npm package — that is the `files` field in package.json, which ships `dist/`
# only — and it does not touch a clone, a fetch or a CI checkout, since those are git
# operations rather than archives. The test suite and the workflows excluded below are
# therefore still there for anyone who clones the repository, and every CI gate still
# runs against all of them.
#
# The rule is that an archive should hold what it takes to build and run the server and
# nothing whose only reader is somebody working on the repository itself. So `src`, the
# manifests, the licence, the changelog and the two guides a user needs stay; the rest
# is listed here. `npm ci && npm run build && node dist/index.js` works in an archive;
# `npm test` and `npm run lint` do not, by design.

# The test suite and its fixtures: the bulk of the repository by file count, and of no
# use without the jest and eslint configuration excluded alongside it.
tests/ export-ignore
jest.config.ts export-ignore
.eslintrc.json export-ignore

# Design notes, plans, specs, and vendored copies of upstream SDK documentation. These
# record why the server is built the way it is, which matters when changing it and not
# when running it. The README links one of them; in an archive that link stops resolving
# locally and still resolves on GitHub, which is the better trade for a document whose
# audience is a contributor.
docs/ export-ignore
SPEC.md export-ignore
examples/ export-ignore

# Instructions and process for working on the repository, not for using the server.
CLAUDE.md export-ignore
CONTRIBUTING.md export-ignore

# CI workflows, issue templates and funding metadata: every one of them addresses this
# repository on GitHub, and none can do anything from inside an archive.
.github/ export-ignore

# The dotfiles that only govern the repository, this file included. Its rules have
# already been applied by the time an archive is written, so carrying it along would
# only leave whoever reads the archive thinking files went missing by accident.
.gitattributes export-ignore
.gitignore export-ignore
